From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: pcs: qca-uniphy: keep the reference clock enabled

The UNIPHY reference clock feeds external 2.5G PHYs (e.g. the RTL8221B on
the IPQ5018 QDM530 lan2 port). It is normally enabled only in the PCS
mac_config path, which runs after the boot-time PHY attach - so the PHY
has no reference clock when the MDIO bus is scanned and reads back missing
(-ENODEV), leaving the port permanently down. Mark the reference clock
CLK_IS_CRITICAL so it is enabled from registration, before the MDIO scan.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
+++ b/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
@@ -221,8 +221,15 @@
 	init.parent_data = &pdata;
 	init.num_parents = 1;
 	init.ops = &qca_uniphy_refclk_ops,
-	/* always derive rate from the UNIPHY register */
-	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE,
+	/*
+	 * always derive rate from the UNIPHY register; keep the reference clock
+	 * enabled from registration (CLK_IS_CRITICAL) so the external 2.5G PHY
+	 * that is fed by it (e.g. the RTL8221B on the IPQ5018 QDM530) already has
+	 * its reference clock when the MDIO bus is scanned early in boot - the
+	 * PCS mac_config that would otherwise enable it runs only after the
+	 * boot-time PHY attach, too late, and the PHY reads back missing.
+	 */
+	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE | CLK_IS_CRITICAL,
 
 	uniphy->ref_clk.hw.init = &init;
 	uniphy->ref_clk.uniphy = uniphy;
